Encoding Categorical VariablesLabelEncoder: Maps categories to integers $[0, C-1]$. Warning: Implies a false mathematical order (e.g., $2 > 1$ does not mean 'Dog' > 'Cat'). Use only for binary targets or naturally ordered data.OneHotEncoder / pd.get_dummies(): Creates binary dummy columns ($1$ or $0$) for each unique class. Eliminates false ordering but suffers from the "curse of dimensionality" if high cardinality is present. Avoid dummy variable trapping by setting drop_first=True.OrdinalEncoder: Manually maps categorical levels to ordered numbers when true hierarchies exist (e.g., 'Low' -> 0, 'Medium' -> 1, 'High' -> 2).Feature Scaling TechniquesStandardScaler: Standardizes features by removing the mean and scaling to unit variance: $x' = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}$. Ideal for algorithms assuming zero-centered, normally distributed inputs (e.g., SVM, Linear Regression, Neural Networks).MinMaxScaler: Scales values into a fixed range: $x' = \frac{x - x_{min}}{x_{max} - x_{min}}$. Compresses data into the range $[0, 1]$. Highly sensitive to outliers.RobustScaler: Uses median and IQR: $x' = \frac{x - \text{median}}{IQR}$. Highly resistant to outlier influence.Normalizer: Scales individual samples to have unit $L_2$ norm. Used in text mining or cosine-similarity distance evaluations.Imbalanced Data StrategiesSMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique): Generates synthetic, non-identical instances along the line segments joining k-nearest neighbors of the minority class. Never apply SMOTE to your validation/test set; only apply to your training set to prevent data leakage.Undersampling: Randomly removes majority class samples. Leads to loss of information.Class Weighting (class_weight='balanced'): Forces classification models to penalize mistakes on the minority class more severely during loss minimization.Feature Engineering & Validation PipelinesBinning: pd.cut divides continuous values into equal-width intervals; pd.qcut splits values into equal-size quantiles (equal number of data points per bin).Datetime Extraction: Convert standard formats and extract .dt.year, .dt.month, .dt.day, .dt.hour, and .dt.dayofweek to provide explicit cyclic indicators to your models.Validation Splits:train_test_split(..., stratify=y, random_state=42): Setting stratify=y guarantees that the training and testing sets preserve the exact class ratio of the original dataset.StratifiedKFold: A variation of K-Fold cross-validation that returns stratified folds, maintaining the target class ratios across each of the $K$ iterations.Advanced Data Visualization and AnalysisMatplotlib Object-Oriented InterfaceConcept Explanation: While Matplotlib's global state-based API (plt.plot) is common, it is highly discouraged for production pipelines. The Object-Oriented API is the robust alternative. You explicitly construct a Figure canvas container and one or more Axes subplots objects. Calls are then made directly to the respective Axes instances, allowing for total control over independent visual hierarchies.Real-World Use: Creating multi-panel charts to display sensor signals, Fourier frequencies, and target predictions side-by-side within a single figure container.Every Relevant Function/Command with Exact Syntax:fig, ax = plt.subplots(nrows=1, ncols=1, figsize=None): Initialize a figure and an array of axes subplots.ax.plot(x, y, label=None, color=None, linestyle=None, marker=None): Draw lines on the axis.ax.scatter(x, y, s=None, c=None, marker=None): Create a scatter plot.ax.bar(x, height, width=0.8, bottom=None) / ax.barh(y, width, height=0.8): Generate vertical or horizontal bars.ax.hist(x, bins=None, density=False): Compute and plot a histogram.ax.boxplot(x, vert=True, patch_artist=False): Create a box and whisker plot.ax.set_title(label, loc='center'): Set the title of the subplot.ax.set_xlabel(xlabel) / ax.set_ylabel(ylabel): Set axis labels.ax.legend(*args, **kwargs): Place a legend on the axes.ax.annotate(text, xy, xytext=None, arrowprops=None): Annotate the point xy with text.fig.savefig(fname, dpi=None, bbox_inches='tight'): Save the active figure to disk, cropping white margins.Code Example:import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
